[Applause]  from the moment he took  office it was clear the administration  of donald trump  would be very different from this day  forward  a new vision will govern our land  from this day forward it's going to be  only america first  it didn't take long for the united  states allies to realize that meant they  were now dealing  with the most disruptive president since  world war ii  straining the system of international  order built over  75 years trump moved quickly  to dismantle the achievements of the  obama administration  at odds with all his closest allies he  pulled out of the paris climate deal  believe me this is not what we need and  then the iran nuclear agreement this was  a horrible one-sided deal  later in the midst of the pandemic he  withdrew the u.s from the world health  organization  today terminating our relationship with  the world health organization  not only did he pull the us out of the  u.n human rights council  the hypocritical and self-serving  organization  trump rarely ever stood up for human  rights and at times he seemed more  comfortable  with the us's traditional adversaries  than its closest allies  we now know from some of his advisers he  came close to pulling out of the us's  most important military alliance in nato  summit meetings like this one in  brussels were awkward events with  everyone else  treading on eggshells to avoid the ire  of the impulsive  often ill-informed u.s leader  most u.s allies around the world are now  breathing a sigh of relief  despite the drama after november's  election  president-elect joe biden's foreign  policy is easy to summarize  reverse direction re-sign  agreements and work collaboratively with  allies  america is back ready to lead the world  not retreat from it once again sit at  the head of the table  ready to confront our adversaries and  not  reject our allies ready to stand up for  our values  the relationship the presidents before  trump built with their allies  was based on trust it's a commodity that  takes a long time to grow but is easy to  lose  and that's the problem for the biden  administration the us's closest allies  will be looking over their shoulder  wondering whether in four years time  trump will be back and if it's not him  it could be another president with  trump-like  america first policies james bay's  al-jazeera washington okay let's get  more on that story for you joining us  live on the newshour al jazeera senior  political analyst marwan bashara  good to talk to you again foreign policy  narrative comes down to personal  skills doesn't it interpersonal skills  so does that mean that mr biden's going  to get on with  everyone because he's been around the  block for so long now  well certainly his 48-year experience in  politics  and in foreign affairs does give him  an an extra asset if you will over both  his predecessor and his predecessor's  predecessor  meaning president obama and president  trump he does have the experience  there's no doubt both in washington and  in the world and i think that's going to  serve him really well  to do the things that he said that he  will do  uh he will he will move in confidently  uh he will as it were uh you know hit  the hit the ground  running and that's why as we've heard  from various reports in  his first day in office he will be  signing a whole number of uh  executive orders perhaps 12 of them  including ending the banner muslim  muslim  muslim majority countries to travel the  united states and a whole bunch of  others  that are important for him to restore  that faith in america to restore  america's greatness or  if i may use the expression peter to  make america great again  after its decline under trump marwan you  can say that one time and one time only  we'll let you away with it this time  do you think his focus though will be  led by ideology  or will it be led by issues i mean you  and i are old enough to remember say  jimmy carter jimmy carter liked to  cherry-pick  issues around the world much the  annoyance  of his own political base sometimes he  put human rights at the center  of his international the international  aspect of his administration  will mr biden have to do the same kind  of thing  well that's the thing president carter  for example was that the total outsider  that he was a you know little known  governor of georgia  uh not much known outside the state uh  until he really broke out of  after the disastrous years of of nixon  right  what we have here is something  completely different biden is the  antithesis of  an outsider he is the ultimate insider  and that's why i think it's going to be  less about ideology and more about the  continuity  of american leadership in the world of  american  leadership of the liberal order in the  world which means  mainstream liberal foreign policy  which means restoring as biden calls it  the spirit of america and what is the  spirit of america  well it is civility at least claiming to  respect human rights  not to be such a mean president as uh  president trump was known  to be so in order to restore what biden  likes to call  american leadership in the world he's  going to have to restore  america's alliances around the world  that again  president trump has trampled all over  and in order to do that  he's going to have to have some program  of some sort for the time being what we  know it's going to be the antithesis  of trump meaning yes building alliances  going back to some of the important  agreement including climate change the  iran nuclear deal  and perhaps some new nuclear deals with  the likes of russia and china  re-assert america's leadership over  international institutions that's sort  of a continuity  of mainstream liberal american  policy which i think is is in some way  ideological but it is a continuity of  almost  half a century of that okay if he does  score some  real international successes here when  he gets beyond  the blitzkrieg of executive orders  unpicking the early days of the trump  administration  how much of that will be tinged with or  driven by  nostalgia not nostalgia in the united  states but nostalgia  around the world because there does seem  to be a genuine  need for a more thoughtful a kinder  gentler washington when it comes to how  it  interacts with other heads of government  around the planet  well yes and i think that's a bit of  where the paradox  lies on the one hand america's allies  notably notably in europe  and southeast asia would like a sort of  the return of america the restoration of  american leadership because that does  support them against  their nemesis whether it's china and the  south china sea  or whether it is russia when it comes to  europe but just to be clear  america's policy has not always been  kind and nice and wonderful  just you know our recent memory we  recall the wars in iraq and the wars in  afghanistan and so on and so forth  america has committed  more than its share uh you know of wars  and  and crimes and so on and so forth around  the world which of course president  obama admitted uh as as much  so in so many ways yes it is about  historical  leadership but in so many other ways to  restore credibility  that's going to take time because i  think the europeans for example  have already made their peace with the  fact that they're going to have to go  it alone if need be that  whatever the future relations with  washington it can it cannot go back to  the pre-trump years  only that europe is going to have to be  treated with mutual respect  and mutual interest that germany and  france and europe as a whole  has uh you know super power interest as  it were  and so they're not going to trust just  the words  of biden they're not going to trust  words in general they're going to have  to  look and they will look be looking for  deeds  meaning not just executive orders but  how  biden will rebuild alliances how he  how will he go back and lead and support  financial and otherwise major  international institutions  like the unesco like the international  human rights council  and likely like the climate change  initiatives and so on and so forth  it does look from that for example let's  say climate change  he already put an emissary and not just  anyone  john kerry which which you know if  if if a if foreign policy is decided by  appointments clearly some of his  appointments  do signal a certain seriousness in  tackling some of the  important global challenges facing  america and the world  i suspect we'll talk about this a lot  over the coming few hours a few days as  well marwan many thanks mawan bashara  there  our senior political analyst

